.TH MXFS 5 "2026-03-10" "MXFS 0.10.0" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
mxfs \- Multinode XFS shared/clustered filesystem
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mount \-t mxfs
.RI [ \-o
.IR options ]
.I device directory
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mxfs
is a high-performance clustered filesystem that enables concurrent
multi-node read/write access to a single shared block device.  It reads
and writes the XFS on-disk format directly through its own DLM-aware
caching engine, delivering native XFS compatibility with full cluster
coordination.
.PP
MXFS is implemented as a single Linux kernel module
.RB ( mxfs.ko )
with no userspace daemon required.  All cluster coordination
(distributed locking, peer discovery, lease management, heartbeating,
and fencing) runs in-kernel.
.SS Architecture
The filesystem consists of three layers:
.IP \(bu 3
.B libmxfs
\(em portable C core library implementing XFS format I/O, DLM-aware
block/inode/directory caches, allocation, journaling, and cluster
services.
.IP \(bu 3
.B PAL
(Platform Abstraction Layer) \(em thin layer providing OS-specific
primitives (block I/O, threads, sockets, memory, time).
.IP \(bu 3
.B Linux VFS frontend
\(em translates VFS operations into libmxfs API calls.
.SS DLM Transports
MXFS supports two DLM (Distributed Lock Manager) transports, named in a configuration
by class and method (see
.I docs/attachment-methods.md
in the source tree):
.TP
.B CAW (Compare-And-Write), disk/caw
Disk-based locking: the lock state lives on the shared LUN and is claimed
with SCSI COMPARE AND WRITE.  No master node \(em every node can acquire
and release locks independently.  Requires a storage target that
implements COMPARE AND WRITE atomically.  Selected with
.BR force_transport=0 .
.TP
.B TCP, net/mesh
Network-based locking with per-resource lock mastering spread across the
members.  The default
.RB ( force_transport=1 ).
.PP
Both transports fence a dead node with SCSI persistent reservations on the
shared LUN, so both need storage that implements them, and both read
through the LUN with SCSI FUA reads for coherency.  Released cluster sizes
are 2, 4, 8 and 16 nodes on either transport, on one iSCSI path per node or
through dm-multipath; two nodes on DRBD dual-primary, which needs no shared
LUN and fences through a fence authority instead, are released on TCP.  See
the README.
.SS Three-Layer Fencing (CAW DLM only)
.IP 1. 3
.B SCSI-3 Persistent Reservations
\(em hardware-level access control on the shared block device.
.IP 2. 3
.B On-disk heartbeat
\(em per-node heartbeat sectors with ~62-second failure detection.
.IP 3. 3
.B DLM lock revocation
\(em stale locks from dead nodes are purged automatically.
.SS Features
.IP \(bu 3
Per-inode lock caching (locks held until BAST, eviction, or unmount)
.IP \(bu 3
AG (allocation group) affinity to minimize cross-node contention
.IP \(bu 3
Zero-config UDP multicast peer discovery (239.66.83.1:7601)
.IP \(bu 3
Per-node write-ahead journal with crash recovery
.IP \(bu 3
Extended attributes (xattrs)
.IP \(bu 3
POSIX ACLs
.IP \(bu 3
.BR fallocate (2)
.IP \(bu 3
.BR mmap (2)
.IP \(bu 3
.BR splice (2)
.IP \(bu 3
SEEK_HOLE / SEEK_DATA
.IP \(bu 3
Filesystem freeze/thaw for LVM snapshots
.IP \(bu 3
.BR sync (2)
/
.BR fsync (2)
.SH MOUNT OPTIONS
The following mount options are recognized by the
.B mxfs
filesystem type.  Options are specified as a comma-separated list
after the
.B \-o
flag.
.TP
.BI peer= addr
An IPv4 address to reach in addition to multicast discovery; repeat the
option for more than one
.RB ( peer=10.0.1.5,peer=10.0.2.7 ).
Multicast discovery runs as usual, and every discovery announcement, lease
heartbeat and, on the CAW transport, BAST nudge is also sent by unicast to
each listed address.  Nobody is dropped.  Use it for a node the multicast
group cannot reach, such as one on another network: that node must list every
member of the cluster, and every member must list it, since neither hears the
other's multicast.
.TP
.BI peers= addr [ / addr ...]
The cluster's IPv4 addresses, separated by
.B /
(the option string itself is split on commas).  Discovery announcements,
lease heartbeats and, on the CAW transport, BAST nudges are sent by unicast
to each listed address instead of the multicast group; no multicast or
broadcast is sent, and datagrams or DLM connections from unlisted addresses
are dropped.  The list may include the node's own address.  Every node of a
cluster must use the same list, or none.
.B peer=
addresses given with it join the same exclusive list, and a repeated
.B peers=
adds to it.  Default: multicast discovery on
.BR 239.66.83.1 .
.TP
.BI cluster= name
The cluster this node belongs to.  Must equal the name recorded on the
filesystem by
.B mkfs.mxfs \-c
or
.BR mxfs_admin (8)
.BR \-c ;
a named filesystem mounted without it, an unnamed one mounted with it, or a
different name, is refused before any cluster traffic or write.  A name is
1\(en63 characters of
.BR A\-Z\ a\-z\ 0\-9\ .\ _\ \- .
.TP
.B noatime
Do not update inode access times on reads.  This is the
.B default
for MXFS, as atime updates on shared storage trigger journal
transactions and device flushes that cause significant read performance
regression.
.TP
.B relatime
Update atime only if it is older than mtime or ctime, or more than 24
hours old.  On MXFS, this option is treated as
.B noatime
for performance.  Use
.B strictatime
to force per-read atime updates.
.TP
.B nodiratime
Do not update directory access times.
.TP
.B strictatime
Update atime on every read.  Overrides the default noatime behavior.
Warning: may reduce read performance by up to 20% on shared storage.
.SH MODULE PARAMETERS
Settings that apply to every MXFS mount on the host are parameters of the
.B mxfs
module (\fBmodprobe mxfs\fR \fIname\fR\fB=\fR\fIvalue\fR, or
.IR /sys/module/mxfs/parameters/ ).
.B modinfo mxfs
lists them all; the ones an administrator is most likely to set:
.TP
.BI force_transport= N
DLM transport a new cluster forms on: 1 = TCP (the default, and what the
release packages set), 0 = CAW (locks on the shared device via SCSI COMPARE
AND WRITE; a device without it is refused at mount).  It only chooses when
a cluster forms: a force_transport=0 mount of a volume that already has
members joins their transport, and a force_transport=1 mount of a volume
with CAW members is refused.  Both are released for clusters of two, four,
eight and sixteen nodes.
.TP
.BI cache_mem_pct= N
Percentage of host RAM available as the total cache budget (1\(en50,
default 10).
.TP
.BI cache_mem_max_mb= N
Hard cap on the total cache budget in MB (default 16384).
.TP
.BI inode_cache_max= N " , block_cache_max=" N
Per-mount cache caps; 0 sizes them from
.BR cache_mem_pct .
.TP
.BI dead_timeout_ms= N
Dead-node detection window in milliseconds (0 = the 62 s default).
.TP
.BI dirshard_mkdir_enable= 0|1
Allow directory sharding's mkdir ioctl (default 0).  Experimental; also
needs a filesystem made with
.BR "mkfs.mxfs \-D" .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/mxfs/node.uuid
Per-node UUID file.  If not present, a UUID is generated and written
on first mount.  Used to derive the 32-bit node ID via FNV-1a hash.
.TP
.I /etc/modules-load.d/mxfs.conf
Module auto-load configuration.  Contains "mxfs" to load the kernel
module at boot.
.TP
.I /usr/sbin/mkfs.mxfs
Filesystem format tool.
.TP
.I /usr/sbin/chk_mxfs
Filesystem integrity checker.
.TP
.I /usr/sbin/fsck.mxfs
Symlink to chk_mxfs for fstab fsck dispatch.
.TP
.I /usr/sbin/resize_mxfs
Filesystem resize tool.
.SH EXAMPLES
Format and mount a shared iSCSI LUN:
.PP
.RS
.nf
mkfs.mxfs /dev/sdb
insmod mxfs.ko
mount -t mxfs /dev/sdb /mnt/shared
.fi
.RE
.PP
Use the TCP DLM transport (for storage without SCSI CAW), set before the
first mount on each node:
.PP
.RS
.nf
modprobe mxfs force_transport=1
mount -t mxfs /dev/sdb /mnt/shared
.fi
.RE
.PP
Mount a named cluster whose nodes are listed explicitly (no multicast):
.PP
.RS
.nf
mount -t mxfs -o cluster=prod,peers=10.0.0.11/10.0.0.12 /dev/sdb /mnt/shared
.fi
.RE
.PP
Add to
.I /etc/fstab
for automatic boot mount:
.PP
.RS
.nf
/dev/sdb  /mnt/shared  mxfs  _netdev  0  0
.fi
.RE
.PP
The
.B _netdev
option ensures the filesystem is mounted after network interfaces are
up, which is required for iSCSI devices and DLM networking.
.SH SUPPORTED STORAGE
A shared SCSI LUN that every node reaches with an initiator of its own and
that implements SCSI persistent reservations, including PREEMPT AND ABORT
(and COMPARE AND WRITE for CAW).  A device that cannot be fenced is refused
at mount.
.IP \(bu 3
.B iSCSI, one path per node
\(em verified (the configurations' attachment
.BR direct ).
.IP \(bu 3
.B iSCSI through dm-multipath, two paths per node
\(em verified, with paths taken away under load (attachment
.BR mpath ;
use the settings in tools/mpath_settings.sh).
.IP \(bu 3
.B DRBD dual-primary, two nodes, no shared LUN
\(em verified (attachment
.BR drbd ,
TCP only).  DRBD has no SCSI reservations; MXFS fences through the site's
fence authority and admits the mount through its DRBD witness.  See the
README, "DRBD dual-primary".
.IP \(bu 3
.B Hypervisor SCSI passthrough
\(em not verified (attachment
.BR pass ).
.IP \(bu 3
.B Fibre Channel, SAS
\(em not verified.
.IP \(bu 3
.B NVMe, NVMe-oF, virtio-blk, md RAID
\(em not supported: none carries SCSI persistent reservations, and MXFS
refuses to mount them.
.SH KERNEL COMPATIBILITY
MXFS supports Linux kernels 5.x through 6.17 with compile-time version
guards for API differences.  Released, each verified from the release
packages: Proxmox VE 9, RHEL/AlmaLinux/Rocky 9.8, Ubuntu 24.04 and Debian 13,
on the kernels the README lists.
